DQL — Data Query Language
Definition

DQL (Data Query Language) is a part of SQL used to retrieve or query data from one or more database tables.

The main DQL command is:

SELECT

Note: Some SQL references classify SELECT simply as a SQL query statement rather than a separate DQL category. In beginner courses, it is commonly taught under DQL.

Why do we use DQL?

Suppose a company has thousands of employee records.
We may want to find:

All employees
Employees from IT department
Employees earning more than ₹50,000
Highest salary
Average salary
Employees from Pune

Instead of manually checking the table, we can use SQL queries to retrieve exactly the required data.

SELECT: The SELECT statement is used to retrieve data from a table.

Syntax:
SELECT column1, column2
FROM table_name;
Example
SELECT Name, Salary
FROM Employees;

This returns the Name and Salary columns from the Employees table.

SELECT All Columns
SELECT *
FROM Employees;

* means all columns.

Example.,
If the table contains:

Employee_ID	Name	Department	Salary
101	Rahul	IT	50000
102	Priya	HR	45000
103	Amit	Sales	60000

Query:
SELECT *
FROM Employees;

returns all three columns and all records.

1. SELECT with WHERE

We can filter the records using WHERE.

Syntax
SELECT column1, column2
FROM table_name
WHERE condition;
Example
SELECT Name, Salary
FROM Employees
WHERE Salary > 50000;

This returns employees whose salary is greater than 50,000.

2. SELECT with ORDER BY

We can sort the result.

SELECT Name, Salary
FROM Employees
ORDER BY Salary DESC;

DESC → Highest to lowest

ASC → Lowest to highest

3. SELECT with DISTINCT

DISTINCT removes duplicate values from the result.

SELECT DISTINCT Department
FROM Employees;

If the table contains:

IT
HR
IT
Sales
HR

the result will be:

IT
HR
Sales

4. SELECT with LIMIT — MySQL
LIMIT is used to restrict the number of rows returned.

SELECT *
FROM Employees
LIMIT 5;

This returns the first 5 records.

DQL Example — Real World

An e-commerce company wants to find products costing more than ₹1,000.

SELECT Product_Name, Price
FROM Products
WHERE Price > 1000;

The database returns only products that satisfy the condition.
